  CSRF = Cross-Site Request Forgery (if enabled it not allows -> post, put,delete) by default enabled
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The basic problem is:
A malicious website tries to make your browser send a request to another website where you are already logged in.

Why does Spring Security enable CSRF by default?
Spring Security assumes that your application may be a browser-based application using cookies/session authentication.


Authentication asks:
Who are you?

CSRF protection asks:
Was this state-changing request intentionally made by the legitimate application/user?

What is a session?
A session is a way for the server to remember an authenticated user across multiple requests.

                                 LOGIN
                                   ↓
                             Authentication
                                   ↓
                            Authentication SUCCESS
                                   ↓
                               Session
                                   ↓
                           JSESSIONID = ABC123
                                   ↓
                               Browser

                           later
                           ------

                               Browser
                                  ↓
                               Cookie: JSESSIONID=ABC123
                                  ↓
                               Server
                                  ↓
                               Find session ABC123
                                  ↓
                               Find Authentication
                                  ↓
                               Ram is authenticated
                                  ↓
                               Authorization
                                  ↓
                               Controller




STATELESS means:
Spring Security should not use an HTTP session to remember authentication between requests.


